
00:00:55.682 --> 00:01:00.415
<v Kieran> So Jane, you'll be off to Denmark soon to do your work placement.

00:01:00.415 --> 00:01:07.847
<v Jane> Yes, I'm really looking forward to it. And I've just started packing up all my books to put in storage.

00:01:07.847 --> 00:01:09.938
<v Kieran> Well, I hope they don't get spoiled.

00:01:09.938 --> 00:01:15.727
<v Jane> It's OK. My grandfather works in a bookshop, and he told me how to pack them.

00:01:15.727 --> 00:01:17.243
<v Kieran> Oh, that's helpful.

00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:22.960
<v Jane> He says you have to support the spine, otherwise the paper can come away from the cover.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.615
<v Kieran> Yeah, that's obvious.

00:01:24.615 --> 00:01:38.863
<v Jane> He also told me to pack them flat in the box, not on their side. Again, because they can bend, and if you leave them like that for, say, a year, it's quite hard to get them back to their normal shape.

00:01:38.863 --> 00:01:44.882
<v Kieran> Well, it's pretty clear that ruins them, but a lot of people just can't be bothered to protect their books.

00:01:44.882 --> 00:01:50.061
<v Jane> He always says it's such a shame that publishers don't use better quality paper.

00:01:50.061 --> 00:01:53.444
<v Kieran> It's the acid in the paper that causes the problem, isn't it?

00:01:53.444 --> 00:02:01.682
<v Jane> Yeah, that's why old books go yellow. You know, some of the books my grandfather's given me are like... that already.

00:02:01.682 --> 00:02:02.328
<v Kieran> Oh.

00:02:02.328 --> 00:02:08.106
<v Jane> I should dump them really if they're going to deteriorate further, but I'd feel bad.

00:02:08.106 --> 00:02:12.513
<v Jane> They'll always remind me of him. He's quite a collector, you know.

00:02:12.513 --> 00:02:14.419
<v Kieran> Well, if they're important to you.

00:02:14.419 --> 00:02:17.183
<v Jane> Yeah, I'd regret just throwing them away.

00:02:17.183 --> 00:02:25.203
<v Kieran> You know, maybe it's because I was taught to treasure books, but I hate seeing students force open the pages of paperbacks.

00:02:25.203 --> 00:02:28.259
<v Kieran> They press so hard they end up breaking the spine.

00:02:28.259 --> 00:02:35.643
<v Jane> I know. But unfortunately, paperbacks aren't designed to last a long time, and people know that.

00:02:35.643 --> 00:02:38.191
<v Jane> Hardbacks aren't quite as weak.

00:02:38.191 --> 00:02:40.300
<v Kieran> Yeah, they're different, I suppose.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:44.189
<v Kieran> But I still don't think people value hardbacks like they used to.

00:02:44.189 --> 00:02:49.201
<v Jane> Well, they aren't decorative, are they, like other objects?

00:02:49.201 --> 00:02:54.620
<v Jane> Plus, nowadays, people don't keep them out on shelves as much as they used to.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:03:02.923
<v Kieran> That's such a pity. When I visit someone, if they have, say, A colourful book on a table. It's the first thing I'm drawn to.

00:03:02.923 --> 00:03:10.802
<v Jane> I agree. And book covers can be a work of art in themselves. Some are really eye-catching.

00:03:10.802 --> 00:03:18.478
<v Kieran> I've always been taught to handle books carefully. If you watch someone take a book off a shelf, well, they usually do it wrong.

00:03:18.478 --> 00:03:24.387
<v Jane> My grandfather says you should put your hand right over the top of the book.

00:03:24.387 --> 00:03:32.107
<v Jane> Or, if you can't do that, pull the other books on the shelf aside so that you can hold the whole cover.

00:03:32.107 --> 00:03:33.744
<v Kieran> When did you learn all this?

00:03:33.744 --> 00:03:40.925
<v Jane> He watched me pull a heavy book off the shelf when I was small, and it fell on the floor and broke apart.

00:03:40.925 --> 00:03:41.753
<v Kieran> Oh dear.

00:03:41.753 --> 00:03:44.436
<v Jane> I can still remember it.

00:03:44.436 --> 00:03:46.199
<v Kieran> You know what I really like?

00:03:46.199 --> 00:03:46.681
<v Jane> What?

00:03:46.681 --> 00:03:48.749
<v Kieran> The smell of new books.

00:03:48.749 --> 00:03:49.729
<v Jane> Me too!

00:03:49.729 --> 00:03:56.090
<v Kieran> My parents used to laugh at me when I was a kid because I loved putting books up to my nose, almost as much as reading them.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:58.758
<v Jane> New books aren't cheap, though, are they?

00:03:58.758 --> 00:04:00.860
<v Kieran> I guess we're lucky we can buy them.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:08.355
<v Jane> My grandfather stocks second-hand books as well as new ones, and they don't smell quite as good.

00:04:49.862 --> 00:04:54.689
<v Kieran> I'd love to have a bookshop like your grandfather. What's it like?

00:04:54.689 --> 00:05:03.389
<v Jane> Well, it's quite big. It's got two floors and an attic, and he stocks all kinds of books really.

00:05:03.389 --> 00:05:07.546
<v Kieran> I guess he treasures things like first editions and other rare books.

00:05:07.546 --> 00:05:11.691
<v Jane> Yeah, you might think he'd keep those in the attic or somewhere.

00:05:11.691 --> 00:05:13.589
<v Kieran> So they'd be hidden.

00:05:13.589 --> 00:05:17.383
<v Jane> Yeah, but he likes people to know that he has them.

00:05:17.383 --> 00:05:22.926
<v Jane> So he puts them out in the shop, but makes sure you need a ladder to get them.

00:05:22.926 --> 00:05:25.077
<v Kieran> Right, that would prevent any thefts.

00:05:25.077 --> 00:05:25.857
<v Jane> Uh-huh.

00:05:25.857 --> 00:05:27.659
<v Kieran> Does he stock books for children?

00:05:27.659 --> 00:05:32.312
<v Jane> He does. He particularly likes to encourage kids to read.

00:05:32.312 --> 00:05:39.239
<v Jane> He always says that he used to sit under the stairs as a child with a pile of books and read them all.

00:05:39.239 --> 00:05:41.042
<v Kieran> Is that where he keeps them then?

00:05:41.042 --> 00:05:52.655
<v Jane> Not exactly. He's got a dedicated area on the ground floor with cushions, so that parents can enter with their toddlers, go there, and spend some time reading to them.

00:05:52.655 --> 00:05:53.874
<v Kieran> Ah, cool.

00:05:53.874 --> 00:06:00.480
<v Jane> And then there's a place for pushchairs by the front door. And a cafe, if anyone needs refreshments.

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:01.543
<v Kieran> That's good to know.

00:06:01.543 --> 00:06:07.246
<v Jane> As I said, it's a big shop. And there's a storage area out the back as well.

00:06:07.246 --> 00:06:10.839
<v Kieran> Oh? What does he keep there? Books he wants to throw away.

00:06:10.839 --> 00:06:18.373
<v Jane> He hardly ever throws anything away. He just leaves unwanted books by the front door for customers to take.

00:06:18.373 --> 00:06:20.343
<v Kieran> Well, that's very nice.

00:06:20.343 --> 00:06:27.281
<v Jane> Yeah. And books people or institutions have requested, they all go at the far end.

00:06:27.281 --> 00:06:28.173
<v Kieran> Oh.

00:06:28.173 --> 00:06:33.344
<v Jane> He thinks it's best to keep these out of the main shopping area as they're boxed and new.

00:06:33.344 --> 00:06:35.775
<v Kieran> Did you get your course books from him?

00:06:35.775 --> 00:06:43.045
<v Jane> Naturally. He stocks books for a lot of the colleges. He used to keep these books on the first floor.

00:06:43.045 --> 00:06:54.469
<v Jane> But now there's a new university in my hometown. He's moved them downstairs to attract the students. They're actually part of the coffee shop on low shelves all around it.

00:06:54.469 --> 00:06:58.521
<v Kieran> Pretty sensual then. You'll have to take me there sometime.
